    "description": "Background of IRC: The International Rescue Committee (IRC) helps people whose lives and livelihoods are shattered by conflict and disaster to survive, recover, and gain control of their future. Founded in 1933 at the request of Albert Einstein, the IRC works with people forced to flee from war, conflict and disaster and the host communities that support them, as well as with those who remain within their homes and communities. At-work today in over 40 countries and 22 U.S. cities, we restore safety, dignity and hope to millions who are uprooted and struggling to endure. IRC leads the way from harm to home. Began work in Ethiopia, 1999 in response to severe drought that happened in Ethiopia’s Somali region. Currently, providing assistance in ten different regions implementing emergency and development programs in health, water and sanitation, economic recovery and development (ERD), women’s protection and empowerment, education, child protection, and multi-sectoral emergency response. The IRC has a focus on narrowing the gender gap in humanitarian aid and ensuring that we contribute to gender equality, diversity, and inclusion in both our programs and operations by working to establish a context where our clients (including those from minority groups) enjoy the same rights and opportunities as well as an equal access to services. The IRC also has a strong commitment to creating an equitable and inclusive culture, where safeguarding is upheld in our workplace and programs. We are determined to protect our clients and staff from safeguarding violations through prevention and, where misconduct is alleged, to address it without fear or favor. Job Overview: Under the direct supervision of the Field Coordinator & project lead and the technical supervision of the ERD Coordinator, the livelihood Manager provides leadership, strategic direction, and oversight for protection programming implemented through the Harar Field Office in Oromia Region. The position is responsible for ensuring quality project implementation, staff management, stakeholder coordination, grant compliance, and adherence to IRC policies, safeguarding, and humanitarian standards. The Livelihood Manager provides technical support and supervision of the ERD teams under Harar field office working on the BRIDGE project to ensure quality program delivery on scope, time and budget and the services are client-centered, safe, ethical, and aligned with IRC and donor a requirement. The position will promote adaptive management, accountability to affected populations, gender equality, social inclusion, safeguarding, environmental sustainability, and conflict-sensitive programming across all project components. Job Purpose The IRC is implementing the Irish Aid-funded BRIDGE Project (Building Resilience, Inclusion, and Durable Growth for Empowerment of Women, Youth, and Other Shock-Affected Communities) in East Hararghe and Gambella Regions of Ethiopia. The Project Manager will lead the implementation of the project in East Hararghe, ensuring integrated delivery of interventions that strengthen food security, resilient livelihoods, climate adaptation, economic recovery, financial inclusion, social cohesion, and community resilience. The position will provide overall leadership to project staff and partners while maintaining strong coordination with government stakeholders, communities, private-sector actors, and donor representatives. The Project Manager will ensure achievement of project objectives through effective planning, resource management, quality assurance, risk management, learning, and evidence-based decision-making. Key Responsibilities Project Leadership and Management Provide overall leadership and strategic direction for project implementation. Ensure timely and quality delivery of project activities, outputs, and outcomes against approved plans and targets. Develop and monitor annual work plans, procurement plans, implementation schedules, and staffing plans. Ensure project implementation complies with donor requirements, IRC policies, and government regulations. Oversee risk identification, mitigation, and adaptive management processes. Promote integration across livelihood, climate resilience, financial inclusion, peacebuilding, and social cohesion activities Technical Oversight and Quality Assurance Provide technical oversight and quality assurance for all project interventions. Ensure interventions are evidence-based, gender-responsive, market-oriented, climate-resilient, and conflict-sensitive. Support technical teams in designing and implementing best-practice approaches in: Climate-smart agriculture Livestock development Natural Resource Management Irrigation Financial inclusion Market systems development Peacebuilding and social cohesion Ensure learning and adaptation are incorporated into implementation Financial and Grant Management Lead project budget planning, forecasting, and expenditure monitoring. Ensure compliance with donor, IRC, procurement, and finance policies. Review Budget versus Actual (BVA) reports and implement corrective actions where required. Monitor burn rates and ensure cost-effective use of project resources. Identify and manage financial risks Monitoring, Evaluation, Accountability and Learning (MEAL) Ensure implementation of robust monitoring and evaluation systems. Track progress against project indicators and targets. Review monitoring data and use findings to improve program quality and decision-making. Ensure timely completion of baseline studies, assessments, evaluations, and learning exercises. Promote documentation of best practices, lessons learned, and success stories. Ensure community feedback mechanisms are functional and responsive. Partnership, Coordination and Representation Serve as the primary project focal point with government counterparts, partners, donors, and relevant stakeholders. Strengthen collaboration with line ministries, community institutions, private-sector actors, and financial service providers. Represent IRC in coordination forums, technical working groups, and project review meetings. Promote strategic partnerships that strengthen sustainability and local ownership. Staff Management and Capacity Building Directly supervise project staff and foster a high-performing team culture. Conduct regular performance reviews, coaching, mentoring, and professional development support. Identify capacity gaps and implement capacity-strengthening initiatives. Promote accountability, teamwork, and adherence to IRC values Safeguarding, Gender and Inclusion Ensure safeguarding principles are integrated throughout project implementation. Promote gender equality and meaningful participation of women and youth. Ensure project interventions are accessible and responsive to marginalized and vulnerable groups. Promote accountability, protection, conflict sensitivity, and Do-No-Harm approaches. Maintain zero tolerance for fraud, corruption, exploitation, discrimination, abuse, and harassment. Safeguarding Responsibilities: - Promote and actively participate in initiatives and efforts to build team engagement, inclusion and cohesion in IRC [team/office] Foster ongoing learning, honest dialogue and reflection to strengthen safeguarding and to promote IRC values and adherence to IRC policies",
