    "description": "Job Title: Special Advisor for Human Resources for Health Location: MoH, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia Reports to: HRDI-LEO Salary: 84197.00 ETB Employment Type & Duration: Full-time, Fixed term Required Number: One (1) Ministry of Health would like to invite competent and interested applicants for the following position. Introduction The Ministry of Health (MOH) of Ethiopia is committed to strengthening the national health system, including developing the human resources for health that improve population health outcomes, achieve universal health coverage (UHC), and advance the Sustainable Development Goals. The Health Sector Development & Investment Plan (HSDIP) serves as a comprehensive roadmap for transforming the Ethiopian health sector, emphasizing the critical role of human resources for health (HRH) in achieving its ambitious goals. Human Resources for Health Strategic and Investment Plan (HRHSIP) document is developed with the aim of ensuring the availability of an adequate number of well-qualified health professionals with the appropriate mix of skills; equitably distributed, motivated, retained, and performing well to achieve UHC. Objective of the position The HRH advisor will provide high-level technical assistance to the MOH to strengthen the HRH system, focusing on; Pre-service training: Enhancing the quality and relevance of health science and medicine education. Organizational development: Strengthening HRH management capacity within the health sector Strengthening the health workforce as part of the Health system: Developing and implementing evidence-based interventions to improve health service delivery and address workforce challenges. Summary of the Position This is a highly technical and strategic position requiring a strong understanding of HRH systems, a proven track record in providing technical assistance and capacity building, and a deep commitment to supporting the development of a resilient and responsive health workforce in Ethiopia. The Senior Specialist will play a vital role in enhancing the MoH's capacity to manage, plan, and develop its human resources effectively, contributing to improved health service delivery and better health outcomes for the Ethiopian people. This position is based in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, and reports to the HRDI-LEO. Main Responsibilities: Pre-Service Training: Expand demand-based specialty and subspecialty training programs and other healthcare professions aligned with service expansion. Operationalize intersectional collaboration with the Ministry of Education (MOE), Ministry of Labor and Skills (MoLS), Education and Training Authority (ETA), Health Science Colleges (HSCs), and Professional Associations (PAs). Coordinate post-basic health science education by focusing on health system demand and priority specialty programs in upgrading Health Extension training. Facilitate the expansion of nursing and Midwifery residency training in collaboration with MOE, HEI, ETA and professional Associations. coordinate the implementation of Nursing Competency Enhancement Program(NCEP) training with the selected higher education institutions. Conduct comprehensive assessments of existing pre-service training programs, including curriculum content, teaching methodologies, assessment methods, faculty capacity, and alignment with national health workforce needs, standards, and competency frameworks. Develop and implement recommendations to align pre-service training with the HSDIP, HRHSIP, and UHC goals, ensuring the curricula are responsive to the evolving needs of the health system. Provide technical support for the development and implementation of new pre-service training curricula and training materials, incorporating innovative teaching approaches, technology-based learning tools, and blended learning methodologies. Facilitate capacity-building activities for pre-service training institutions, including faculty development programs, curriculum design workshops, mentorship initiatives, and resource mobilization to enhance their capacity to deliver high-quality training aligned with national standards. Support the expansion of capacity of existing training institutions and create partnerships with national and international bodies to increase the number of available training positions for postgraduate specialty and sub-specialty programs. Coordinate the quality assurance mechanism and accreditation process of health science education in collaboration with ETA, MOE, HLI and PAs. Main Deliverables: Pre-Service Training: In collaboration with MOE and ETA, organize the revision and implementation of post-basic curricula across universities and regional health science colleges. Provided leadership and coordinated accreditation of health science and medicine programs across public and private universities and colleges. Coordinated the Implementation of System Oriented Medical Education (SOME) in medical schools Supported the establishment and strengthening of skills laboratories and simulation centers to enhance practical competencies of health science students through simulation-based training. Supported the strengthening of clinical education programs to enhance the competencies of health science students through practical, competency-based training. Supported the integration of AI-enabled digital learning approaches to enhance the quality and accessibility of health science education. Organizational Development Strengthened organizational capacity within the MoH for effective HRH management, including improved leadership, performance management, organizational culture, and HRIS capabilities, including National Health Workforce Accounts Implementation. Developed and implemented HRH policies and guidelines aligned with national health policies, international best practices, and legal frameworks. Strengthening the workforce as part of the health system: Provided technical support on the integration and implementation of the HRHSIP-2024-2030 with Health Sector Development and Investment Plan II (HSDIP II). Improved health service delivery through enhanced workforce planning, deployment, retention, and equitable distribution of health professionals, particularly in underserved areas. Increased knowledge sharing and collaboration among HRH stakeholders, leading to a more robust national HRH system. Published research findings, policy briefs, and knowledge products to inform HRH decision-making, strengthen national capacity in HRH, and promote evidence-based practice.",
